{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "The code is meant to be thrown away. What matters is how fast you learn whether the idea works, not how good the code is. Accepting AI output based on whether it runs is fine here, as long as no real credentials, customer data, or production systems are involved."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "A demo still mostly needs to work convincingly, but it gets shown to outsiders and often becomes the base for the real product. Skim the core logic and anything touching secrets, external APIs, or data, and test the demo path so it doesn't break in front of stakeholders."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "Real users mean real data, security exposure, and code the team will keep building on. Let AI write most of it, but read auth, payments, data handling, and schema changes line by line, and use tests to check behavior on everything else. The team needs to understand the code well enough to debug it."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "Paying customers and a team that maintains the code make correctness, security, and maintainability matter more than speed. Every AI-written change should get the same line-by-line review as human-written code, with an accountable person who understands it, and should pass existing tests and CI."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 1,
      "rationale": "In regulated or safety-critical systems, a person has to own and be able to justify every line, and many compliance regimes require traceable human authorship and review. Use AI for suggestions, analysis, test ideas, and review help, but have people write or rewrite the code that ships, and keep the normal certification and verification processes."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Read AI-written code more closely as the cost of a hidden defect goes up: skip reading it for throwaway prototypes, review selectively and test early products, review every line in production, and keep people writing the code in critical systems. In practice, set the level by risk, not just by stage. High-risk areas (auth, payments, data migrations, security boundaries) should get at least level 2 at every stage after the prototype, and code from a prototype or demo should be reviewed again before it moves into the MVP or production."
}

